  "decision": {
    "answer": "SUPPORTS",
    "explanation": "The provided evidence from pmid-27404270 explicitly states that in a study of 908 patients with colorectal cancer, BRAF V600E mutations were associated with a shorter survival time (31.1 months vs 41.6 months, p=0.001) and a significantly poorer 3-year survival rate (63.8% vs 87.9%, p<0.0001) compared to BRAF wild-type tumors. This directly supports the claim that BRAF V600E somatic mutations in colorectal cancer are associated with a poor outcome.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-27404270",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 597,
        "quote": "In a study of 908 patients with colorectal cancer, 45 patients had BRAF V600E mutations and 589 patients were BRAF wild type.  BRAF V600E mutations were more likely to be proximal tumors (68.9%, 20.7%; p<0.0001), they were more likely to be poorly differentiated (17.7%, 1.9%; p<0.0001), they were more likely to be mucinous carcinoma type (20.0%, 4.2%; p=0.0003), they were more likely to have lymphatic invasion (77.6%, 49.9%; p=0.0003), and they had a shorter survival time (31.1 mo, 41.6 mo; p=0.001).  The 3-year survival rate was significantly poorer in the V600E group when compared to the "
      },
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-27404270",
        "start": 597,
        "end": 638,
        "quote": "wild type group (63.8%, 87.9%; p<0.0001)."
      }
